// Formative practice quiz for create-edit-and-delete-against-the-api. Low-stakes; answers are visible by design. First draft, not yet reviewed.
$CATEGORY: react/forms-context-and-crud/create-edit-and-delete-against-the-api

::create-edit-and-delete-against-the-api o1 - stringify::You are posting a JavaScript object as JSON with fetch. What must you do?{
=Set body to JSON.stringify of the object and add a Content-Type header#Correct. The body must be a string, and the header says what it is.
~Pass the object as body, because fetch converts it#Not quite. fetch sends the text [object Object] instead.
~Put the object in the URL#Not quite. That is not how a JSON body is sent.
~Call response.json first#Not quite. That parses a response, not the request.
}

::create-edit-and-delete-against-the-api o1 - method choice::Which request removes item 7?{
=DELETE to /api/items/7#Correct. DELETE on one item removes it.
~POST to /api/items/7#Not quite. POST is usually for creating in a collection.
~GET to /api/items/7 with a delete flag#Not quite. GET should only read, and never change data.
~PUT to /api/items#Not quite. That targets the collection rather than item 7.
}

::create-edit-and-delete-against-the-api o2 - after success::A POST succeeds and returns the created item, including its new id. How should the screen update?{
=Add the returned item to a new array in state#Correct. The response has the server values, including the id.
~Push the typed values into the state array#Not quite. That mutates state and has no server id.
~Do nothing, because the list updates itself#Not quite. React does not know the server changed.
~Reload the whole page#Not quite. A full reload loses state, and fetching the list again would be enough.
}

::create-edit-and-delete-against-the-api o2 - failed update::Why check res.ok before updating the list?{
=Otherwise an error response is treated as a successful result#Correct. fetch resolves for a 500, so the screen would claim success.
~Because res.json only works when ok is true#Not quite. res.json can parse error bodies too, which is exactly the trap.
~Because React requires it#Not quite. React knows nothing about your requests.
~To make the request faster#Not quite. It has no effect on speed.
}

::create-edit-and-delete-against-the-api o3 - double click::Users double-click Save and create two records. What is the standard fix?{
=Disable the submit control while the request is in flight#Correct. A second press then cannot send another request.
~Tell users to click once#Not quite. People will still double-click.
~Remove the button#Not quite. They still need a way to save.
~Send the request twice on purpose#Not quite. That guarantees the duplicate.
}
